<header class="top">
  <p class="home"><a href="index.html">← the ladder</a><a href="r17.html">next: 17 turning multiplying into adding →</a></p>
  <h1>Geometry floor</h1>
  <p class="gesture" data-pid="106f56">Rungs 17 to 21. You arrive with slope and area, and with a growing habit of asking what a word really means. Here even the shapes you drew as a child become guesses waiting for proof: what a circle is, what a turn is, what stays put when everything moves. You leave able to say what a shape is, what a move is, and what a geometry keeps.</p>
</header>

<section class="class">
  <div class="eyebrow">The floor · read it through, then climb on</div>

  <h2 class="intro" style="font-size:2.1rem;line-height:1.15;margin:4px 0 14px">Introduction</h2>
  <p data-pid="f8d0b8">Until now the ladder has kept pictures as pictures. It has used the word plane for the place where graphs are drawn, and left distance, shape and turning alone. This floor brings them in, built from what you already have.</p>
  <p data-pid="96067f">Rung 17 finds a function, built from an area, that turns multiplying into adding. Rung 18 lays numbers over the plane, so that a shape becomes a statement about pairs of numbers and a distance becomes something you can compute.</p>
  <p data-pid="6255db">Rung 19 studies moves: slides and turns that carry a shape somewhere else. Moves can be combined, undone, and one move does nothing at all. Rung 20 asks what a move keeps, and finds that a geometry is exactly that: whatever its moves leave unchanged. Rung 21 measures turning as a distance walked round a circle, and finds the sine wave in where the walk ends.</p>
  <p data-pid="d0b698"><b>On this floor, geometry stops being a set of pictures and becomes algebra on pairs of numbers, judged by the moves it allows.</b></p>
  <p data-pid="f4c1a9">On the first floor you met induction under small cases: a small cluster of results points to one rule, and its exceptions can be named and tested again. On this floor that guess gets its fuller name, because geometry is full of it. A shape moved, turned or stretched varies in ways a rule can predict. So from here on it is called induction under the case with predictable variation. A law, met at 5e, is what this kind of induction hands you: one rule, its range, and exceptions that come back each time the test is run again.</p>
  <p data-pid="980373">The old geometry of compass and straightedge is not thrown away. It is rebuilt, and you will see why its old answers were right. Like every floor, this one ends at a beginning: there are more geometries than the one you were taught, and you have only met the first.</p>
  <h3>The rungs of this floor</h3>
  <ul class="floorlist">
    <li><a href="r17.html">17 · turning multiplying into adding</a></li>
    <li><a href="r18.html">18 · drawing with numbers</a></li>
    <li><a href="r19.html">19 · undoing the moves</a></li>
    <li><a href="r20.html">20 · moving the plane</a></li>
    <li><a href="r21.html">21 · rounding an arc</a></li>
  </ul>
  <p data-pid="7f2951">At the top of the floor are its conclusion and its measuring stick. Passing it gives you the title geometer, and the Statistics floor opens above.</p>
</section>

